<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">History of the Patriarchs of Alexandria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The History of the Patriarchs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> is a collection of biographies of the Coptic patriarchs of Alexandria, compiled in Arabic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from the 11th century and traditionally attributed to Severus ibn al-Muqaffa. Drawing on earlier Coptic sources and extended by later continuators, it is a principal source for the Coptic church, for Egypt under Islamic rule and for the Christian kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/history-of-the-patriarchs-of-alexandria.jpg" alt="History of the Patriarchs of Alexandria" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Genaynan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%B3%D9%90%D9%8A%D9%8E%D8%B1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D9%90%D9%8A%D8%B9%D9%8E%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%8F%D9%82%D9%8E%D8%AF%D9%8E%D9%91%D8%B3%D9%8E%D8%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The History of the Patriarchs is the only continuous narrative of the non-Chalcedonian patriarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. Its earliest parts derive mainly from Eusebius and Coptic tradition, but from the 6th century onwards the biographies grow longer and often seem to rest on documents written by eyewitnesses. The account of the 6th-century patriarchs, the Muslim conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the lives of Pope Benjamin I of Alexandria and his four successors down to Pope Simeon I of Alexandria was recorded by George the Archdeacon in the early 8th century. The work also preserves a vivid eyewitness account of the overthrow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marwan-ii">Marwan II</a>, the last Umayyad caliph.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tradition holds that Severus Ibn al-Muqaffaʿ, Coptic bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermopolis">Hermopolis</a>, began compiling the book in Arabic, and that it told the miracle of the moving of the Mokattam Mountain under the Fatimid caliph al-Muizz around 975. Others continued it, among them Michael, bishop of Tinnis, who covered the years 880 to 1046, Mawhub ibn Mansur ibn Mufarrig, deacon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, and Pope Mark III of Alexandria, for 1131 to 1167. Modern scholarship instead credits the composition to Mawhib ibn Mufarrij of Alexandria (c. 1025–1100). Appendices and continuations carry the text to 1894, and one unpublished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> continues it to 1923.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Eusèbe Renaudot published a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> version, Historia patriarcharum alexandrinorum jacobitarum, in 1713. Christian Friedrich Seybold began a scholarly Arabic edition in 1904. Basil Thomas Alfred Evetts edited and translated the first half of the Arabic text into English as the History of the Patriarchs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Church</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in the Patrologia Orientalis series, stopping with the 52nd patriarch, Joseph, who died in 849. Later parts were published and translated by scholars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> led by O. H. E. Burmester.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “History of the Patriarchs of Alexandria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the <b>History of the Patriarchs of Alexandria</b>, King Kyriakos marched into Upper Egypt around 747 to demand the release of the imprisoned Coptic patriarch, and Arab historians report that Nubian forces raided Upper Egypt as far as Akhmim in the 960s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Military</a></li></ul></section>
